joining me now is martin luther king the  third global human rights activist and  of course the son  of martin luther king very good to talk  to you thank you very much indeed for  joining us and  let's talk about that service first of  all what did you make of it quite a  tribute to john lewis  it certainly was in our tradition we  call it an extraordinary home going  where many paid phenomenal tributes  uh certainly three presidents former  presidents  uh as as as well as those who were very  close to  uh the congressman but an extraordinary  homegoing service  we're going to sorely miss his  leadership his moral leadership because  he was not just the conscience  of the congress but perhaps he was even  the conscious of had grown to become the  conscious  of our nation and tell us of course  about his  his work with your father we can see the  the portrait  behind you there just tell us what you  what you witnessed in terms of the  dynamic between them  well first of all as a child uh the  congressman was in and out of our home  so i remember him and because he worked  with my father  uh in many ways as the student  non-violent coordinating committee  uh chairperson and and leader for a  period of time  i knew him then and because he worked  with dr abernathy and  jose williams and andrew young and a  host of others  all of these gentlemen uh harry  belafonte  also uh worked very closely with my dad  became  i like our play uncles so john lewis was  like our play uncle  but as i grew older and ultimately  once he was elected to office i happened  to be elected at the same time  and he served as a city councilman here  in atlanta i served as a fulton county  commissioner  and we worked together over the years of  course he worked with my mother  and continued to work with her once he  went to congress to help  get certain legislation passed over and  over again and my mom did her part to  get the help  lobby for the legislation but in terms  of my father and he  he first my father first met him i think  at 18 years old  dad uh and the organization paid for a  ticket for him to come from troy  uh to montgomery and he had  already been listening to my father's  speeches so he was inspired  by his speeches and his example dad was  of the personification of non-violence  john lewis ultimately personified  non-violence  uh and peacefulness throughout his life  so you really well placed then to give  us an insight  into his character you know we heard  there  some of those eulogies talking about  john lewis how humble he was but how  determined and persistent as well  yes he was he was unyielding  in a sense but he did it in a way that  you had to respect him  regardless of if you agreed with what he  was advocating for  now of course i would be on a side that  agreed with everything  uh that he was advocating there were  others in congress who may not have  agreed  but they respected him and came to  admire  and and love him because he did it in  such a way in the sense  my father taught us how to be  disagreeable without  how to be uh constructive without being  disagreeable so you didn't have to be  hostile  uh yes you were very passionate but you  didn't have to exhibit anger you did not  have to personally  attack someone because they had a  different position  than yours and this this is called  character  this is called integrity uh this is  called  also a vision john lewis shared her  vision  and he kept coming back over and over  and over again  used to say don't give up don't give out  and in a sense don't give in  and he personified all of that open  doors and unfortunately we're still  having challenges around  voting in this 2000 in 2020 we're going  to have  challenges that we have to address  because there is voter suppression  that has been uh voted on all throughout  many of our states and congressman lewis  wanted to make it possible for all of us  to vote we  we need to reenact what we call the  voting rights act  in fact the house of representatives  passed a bill the john  lewis restoration voter registration act  and it is my hope the senate will do  that uh they have not so far but yes it  has happened in  in the house yeah and we heard barack  obama getting very angry  about that but but what does it tell us  then about the current state of race  relations in  the united states that three former  presidents  from all parties attended but of course  the  the current one the serving one did not  so i i think it says that the current  president is operating  exclusively from a from a narrow  political perspective  believing i mean you know you have to  acknowledge the fact that he attacked  john lewis um he attacked the district  uh john lewis did not go to his  inauguration because he did not feel  that he had been elected in a  responsible way  and uh so there have been issues all  along but  the higher issue is that when you talk  about  race relations my father wanted to  eradicate what he called were the triple  evils and my mother in their lives  and those are the evils of poverty  racism and violence  and of course while we've made strides  unfortunately  the president current president has  intentionally attempted to divide people  and the hope is that  many many people will come together we  are seeing the largest civil rights  demonstrations  in the world with people holding signs  saying black lives matter  whether it's in europe or whether it's  in australia or whether it's in south  america  and obviously their demonstrations  consistently going on  here in the united states but these  demonstrations  are demonstrators are holding up signs  that say black lives matter  this we had a new juncture a new phase  john lewis  ambien who also passed on same day they  took their last march together  but what i think will happen or is  happening  is we're still working through race  we're not where we need to be  but we're going to get there because  there are far more people of goodwill  who believe in racial sensitivity than  those who are  segregationists and racists well martin  luther king the third it has been an  honor  and a pleasure talking to you and thank  you for those  insights into the life of a remarkable  man  very good talking to you thank you for  the opportunity
 

